   <text type="letter">
      <body>
         <div type="writingSession">
            <pb n="1"/>
            <opener><dateline><date when="1948-12-18">Dec. 18, 1949.</date></dateline>
               <salute>My dear <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person">Professor
               Tillich</rs>;–</salute>
            </opener>
            <p>Your recent article in Theology Today on the Present Theological Situation was of great
            interest to me. I want to express my appreciation for it.</p>
            <p>It so happened that when I came to read your article I had just finished reading The
               Continuity of the Christian
               Faith<!-- wahrscheinlich gemeint The Continuity of Christian Thought --> by <rs
                  ref="#tillich_person_id__5378" type="person">Alexander Allen</rs>. In the light of
               your position I am wondering what you would think of <rs
                  ref="#tillich_person_id__5378" type="person">Allen</rs>. The two pose a dialectic
               for me which it seems to me must somehow be resolved, nor is the dialectic purely
                  <unclear>accademic</unclear> but rather existential for it has to do with the
               question as to what Christianity essentially is and therefore what we use to
               preach.</p>
            <closer>
               <salute>With all good wishes I am<lb/>cordially yours</salute>
               <signed><rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1645" type="person"
                  >Harry W. Rosengrant</rs></signed>
            </closer>
         </div>
      </body>
   </text>
